Description: Fix cramped periodic table layout on Linux (negative grid gap)
 On GTK, PERIODIC_TABLE_GRID is set to (-7,-7): a *negative* gap
 between every cell of the periodic table's GridBagSizer (element
 buttons, the lanthanide/actinide connector line, and the selected
 element's info text all share this one grid). This looks like a
 leftover compensation for old GTK2 button themes that carried extra
 internal padding; current GTK3 buttons don't have that padding, so
 the negative gap now just crowds everything together with no
 breathing room, most visibly between the button grid and the
 selected element's name/mass text.
 .
 Confirmed by instantiating the dialog, selecting an element, and
 reading widget positions directly: the gap between the Be button's
 right edge and the info text's left edge was 11px before this fix
 (104 to 133 minus button width), consistent with the user's report
 of a cramped layout with text crowding the element boxes. Changed to
 the same (2,2) gap the Windows/other-platform default already uses;
 after the fix the same gap grows to 29px, and the dialog gains
 sensible padding throughout rather than just at this one spot, since
 the change affects the whole shared grid.
